/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LucasHang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LucasHang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LucasHang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LucasHang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LucasHang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2024-01-12T16:17:14Z","updated_at":"2024-01-12T16:17:14Z","body":"> > Hi there,\r\n> > Parallel routes are meant to be used within a shared layout. We do not currently support creating a separate `layout.tsx` file per slot. The valid files for a slot are `error`, `loading`, `default`, and `page`.\r\n> > Could you clarify the use-case behind having a layout for your slot?\r\n> \r\n> the conditional rendering example in the documentation suggests otherwise.\r\n> \r\n> https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/routing/parallel-routes#conditional-routes\r\n> \r\n> in this example you have dashboard and login ... which I need different layouts for.\r\n\r\nI agree that this example creates a false expectative of using parallel routes for complex and ramificated use cases.\r\nBut looking now, I guess that they are trying to show a root path (like \"/home\") that can show login or dashboard given the user auth state, while the other supposable routes are not **under** this layouts (a logic checking authentication would end up redirecting an unathorized user to the root path, that would show login page, for example).\r\n\r\nSo we can imagine something like:\r\n- path \"/home\" (shows dashboard or login)\r\n- path \"/anything\" (shows anything)\r\n\r\nThe layout where parallel routes are defined is under \"home\" folder. Thereby \"/anything\" is a independent route, not under parallel logic.","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vercel/next.js/issues/comments/1889587256/reactions","total_count